2.1.m:
"Those who know ancient lore declare mine to be the lineage of the Sun;
whose names are infinite, purifying to the wise;
whom ascetics call Kṛṣṇa both by color and by name;
whose discus brings terror to the enemies of Brahmā and the foes of the gods;
hearing the sound of whose conch his enemies indeed fell into swoon;
a bow equal to whose bow exists not even among the immortals;
me, the lord of heroes dwelling upon Mount Veṅkaṭa, they declare him to be.
From that mountain ridge, I, surrounded by hunters as my followers,
mounted upon a horse, have come to your forest for the hunt.
Pursued by me, a deer running with the speed of the wind fled away.
Not finding it, looking about the forest, I beheld this beautiful lady.
Drawn by desire I have come here before you; can she indeed be won by me?"
Hearing the words of Kṛṣṇa, enraged they spoke again:
"Before King Ākāśa sees you and binds you in chains
and takes you away, go quickly to your own abode!"
